<section class="panel cp-span-full" id="raids">
    <h2>Raids: Hyjal Summit and the Barrow Deeps</h2>
    <p>Two raids launch with Forever's endgame, unlocking <strong>December 9, 2026</strong>, with new tier sets and a legendary reward Blizzard is "looking forward to revealing in full." The team's ongoing raid philosophy, per lead designers Tim Jones and Michael Nuthals in Kotaku's September 14 interview: every raid tier ships at least one 10-player and one 20-player raid, sometimes a 40-player, with no flex raiding — "always a 10-player raid for groups... every single tier, forever." The September 17 Q&A panel added that there are <strong>no heroic versions of dungeons or raids</strong> — "the difficulty of group content is what it is" — and <strong>no cross-faction raiding</strong>; faction friction is deliberate.</p>
    <table class="fact-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Raid</th><th scope="col">Size</th><th scope="col">Setting</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Hyjal Summit</td><td>20-player</td><td>20-player encounters at one of Warcraft's most important locations — the mountain's summit above the war against the Burning Legion. Beta client lists 13 encounters ending with The Wild King (reported)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>The Barrow Deeps</td><td>10-player</td><td>Planned as a maximum-level 10-player challenge set in the deep caverns below Teldrassil. Beta client lists 8 encounters (reported). Per senior encounter designer Michael Nuthals (The Sun interview), it is the prison vault where Illidan was held, inspired by the Warcraft III "Brothers in Blood" mission: the wardens left and the prisoners now run the prison. It has multiple entrances, one in Hyjal</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Onyxia's Lair</td><td>40-player</td><td>Named by principal designer Kris Zierhut in The Sun's BlizzCon interview as Forever's third raid: "The Hyjal Summit will be for 20 players, and then we'll also be offering Onyx's Lair for 40 players" (transcribed spelling), with tier pieces earnable across all three raids. The Legacy tree's "Conqueror of the Lair" challenge (Defeat Onyxia, per classicwowforever.com's pre-release capture) corroborates it, and Quissy's BlizzCon roundup places Onyxia's Lair in the launch raid progression with the December 9 unlock. Whether it opens December 9 with the others still lacks an official recap confirmation. [Dec 9 timing NEEDS VERIFICATION]</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="meta-info">More raids follow on the roadmap, including a "revamped iconic raid" and a legendary reward still being kept under wraps. Molten Core will open "someday, but not now" — the team cited Molten Core fatigue across Era, Season of Mastery, Season of Discovery and Anniversary (per principal designer Kris Zierhut, The Sun interview).</p>
</section>

<section class="panel cp-span-full" id="pvp">
    <h2>Darkspear Islands Battleground (15v15)</h2>
    <p>The <strong>Darkspear Islands</strong> battleground sits off the coast of Kalimdor and pairs strategic control points with Arathi Basin-style flag captures and defense in a <strong>15 vs 15</strong> format. PvP updates, dungeon quests and new rewards are planned to keep conflict, cooperation and discovery part of Forever's ongoing expansion.</p>
    <p><strong>Ranked progression:</strong> The PvP progression system is built around Classic-style honor earned into ranks, with a rank-14 ceiling meant to be reached within a single season or patch cycle rather than over years (per lead designers Tim Jones and Michael Nuthals, Kotaku interview September 14). Rewards include cosmetic-rank items plus PvP armor that carries stats.</p>
    <p><strong>Battle-ready at the top end:</strong> the beta files show a 537-piece <strong>Premier PvP tier</strong> — 292 epics (level 60, item level 65) and 245 rares (level 55, item level 60) across four armor themes (Magus cloth, Mortarplate plate, Dragonhide and Shadowhide leather), spanning the classic ranks from Champion up to High Warlord — plus a faction ladder pitting <strong>Theramore vs Darkspear</strong> with jewelry tiers at required levels 28/38/48/58, armor at 50/60, care packages and a Darkspear Islands Mark of Honor (per classicwowforever's beta datamine; names and stat mixes are datamined, exact acquisition is not in the files). The September 17 Q&A panel confirmed there is <strong>no rated PvP</strong>: instead a <strong>seasonal Honor track</strong> with its own reward set resets each tier/season, and Blizzard plans to incentivize all PvP activities rather than Alterac Valley farming as the easiest Honor source.</p>
    <img src="https://bnetcmsus-a.akamaihd.net/cms/content_entry_media/RLMTVX61QGEW1789247709428.png" width="1280" height="720" loading="lazy" alt="The Darkspear Islands 15v15 battleground in World of Warcraft: Forever" class="img-round">
</section>

<section class="panel cp-span-full" id="systems">
    <h2>Classes, Systems and Player Choice</h2>
    <p>Class identity stays central to Forever, with new abilities, new race and class combinations (the neutral <strong>Forsaken Paladin</strong> and the Skyborne combinations are the confirmed examples, per the panel recap and warcraft.wiki.gg coverage) and updates "designed to preserve the feel of the original game while opening new possibilities." The stated goal is covering gaps where they exist without erasing what makes each class unique.</p>
    <div class="class-icons" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/classes/warrior.jpg" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy"><img src="/images/classes/paladin.jpg" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy"><img src="/images/classes/hunter.jpg" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy"><img src="/images/classes/rogue.jpg" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy"><img src="/images/classes/priest.jpg" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy"><img src="/images/classes/shaman.jpg" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy"><img src="/images/classes/mage.jpg" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy"><img src="/images/classes/warlock.jpg" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy"><img src="/images/classes/druid.jpg" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy"></div>
    <div class="cp-cols">
        <div>
            <h3>Progression</h3>
            <ul class="plain-list">
                <li><strong>Legacy</strong> — account-wide perks for alternate characters, for players who love leveling and running alts</li>
                <li><strong>Camping</strong> — craft campfires in the outdoor world so other adventurers can stop by, contribute through professions, and share campsite buffs</li>
                <li>New talents, abilities and itemization across the level 1–60 journey</li>
                <li><strong>Class quest lines</strong> — Season of Discovery's class quests carried into Forever, with some new and some expanded; more are planned after launch (per Greenfield, MMORPG.com interview; single source so far)</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
            <h3>Player choice</h3>
            <ul class="plain-list">
                <li>Opt-in <strong>Transmog</strong></li>
                <li>Modern and Classic visual presets</li>
                <li><strong>HD / SD</strong> character model options, with restored Classic animations for HD models. Zierhut (The Sun interview): enabling HD shows all players in HD; player-model NPCs (e.g. a Stormwind guard) change too, while creature models (dragons, gnolls) and world textures do not</li>
<li><strong>First-login style choice</strong> — on first login you pick Classic or Enhanced; the Classic style defaults to disabling transmog and quest tracking and enables SD models, all adjustable later. The HD/SD toggle affects textures only; animations use the modern skeletons for technical reasons (September 17 Q&A recap)</li>
<li><strong>Modern addon API</strong>, blocking addons that effectively play the game for you — plus a <strong>DPS meter, cooldown tracker and swing timer included as default features</strong>. Corroborated and detailed by Icy Veins (September 17): Forever shares Mainline WoW's UI architecture "including the vast majority of APIs available in 12.1.5," per a Blizzard representative's message in the WoW UI Discord (surfaced via WeakAuras), and the built-in damage meter and cooldown manager were confirmed in a Game Informer interview — with the stated goal of preserving addon customization without giving addons "too much power and control over a player's experience." (September 17 Q&A recap + Icy Veins). Greenfield (MMORPG.com interview, September 15) confirms the damage meter is in but says the cooldown manager may miss beta, planned for the near future</li>
                <li>Official <strong>gamepad</strong> support</li>
                <li><strong>Barbershops</strong> coming (per Quissy's BlizzCon roundup of the panels)</li>
                <li><strong>No WoW Token or character boosts</strong> — associate production director Clay Stone told YouTuber Destin there are "no plans for anything like a WoW Token or a boost": the journey matters most and you "get better at the game by playing the game" (via GamesRadar, September 14)</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
            <h3>Rendering</h3>
            <ul class="plain-list">
                <li>New lighting and global illumination</li>
                <li>Volumetric fog and god rays</li>
                <li>Improved water graphics and shadows</li>
                <li>Environmental touches: flowing rivers in Elwynn Forest, mist at Darkshore, moonlight in Ashenvale</li>
                <li>Ray-traced global illumination tuned for minimum spec, real-time sun and moon lighting and shadows, and a new water shader (per wowforeverclassic, official)</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
            <h3>Combat and itemization</h3>
        <ul class="plain-list">
            <li><strong>Unified hit and crit</strong> — spell, melee and ranged hit chance are combined, as are the crit-chance stats; Blizzard says this makes Warrior/Hunter/Rogue tools like Taunts, Poisons and Traps more reliable (per the Deep Dive recap)</li>
            <li><strong>Tank threat is capped at 3–4 targets</strong> — per Blizzard's Forever panel videos (wowforeverclassic, September 17); it matches Consecration hitting hard on the first four targets and the Paladin tank kit</li>
            <li><strong>Paladin rework</strong> — the Deep Dive panel detailed a full Paladin refresh: <em>Holy Strike</em> (instant weapon strike, Holy damage, 12s cooldown) at level 6, Seals no longer consumed by Judgment, <em>Seal of Fury</em> for tanking (fast weapons, small absorb shields, Judgment taunts while active), and <em>Consecration</em> baseline at level 20 (high threat on the first four targets, taunt included). Holy gains Voice of Truth (silence/interrupt immunity), Holy Shock at a 10s cooldown and Light's Vigil; Protection gains Mana on absorbs/blocks and a once-per-minute Judgment reset; Retribution gains seal twisting without a swing-timer add-on. Paladins still lack an interrupt and a slow (per the Deep Dive recap). wowforeverclassic's panel-transcribed reads (September 17) add: Protection's <strong>Templar's Bulwark</strong> grants an absorb shield equal to 100% of maximum health (21-point row), <strong>Reckoning</strong> is reworked to trigger from blocks (40% chance of an extra attack per block), <strong>Shield Specialization</strong> restores 6% mana per block at most once every 3 seconds, <strong>Iron Creed</strong> adds 25% Holy Strike threat and reduces damage taken by 10% for 6s under Righteous Fury, <strong>Divine Favor</strong> moves to the 16-point row, Judgment's panel slide reads 91 mana / 10-yard range / 10s cooldown / instant — and <strong>Undead Paladins start at Bandarion Keep in Tirisfal Glades with their own epic mount quest</strong>. One report calls the tank seal "Seal of Wrath" — a likely naming conflict. Beta numbers (Tavern, September 21, beta-graded): Consecration capped at 4 full-effect targets (75% less beyond), Blessing of Might down ~39%, Divine Shield switched to the Wrath 50%-damage variant, Lay on Hands cooldown 20 minutes, Holy Wrath stuns Undead/Demons 2s, and most improved-talent effects folded baseline</li>
            <li><strong>Warrior anti-mount tech</strong> — a captured "Spearing Strike" talent (Rank 0/1) attacks for 40% weapon damage, +80% vs giants/dragonkin/mounted targets, and dismounts riders (15 Rage, 20s cooldown; pre-release creator footage per Xaryu)</li>
            <li><strong>Itemization rework</strong> — hit and crit unified (above), weapon skill grants less per item, bonus healing now includes one third as much bonus damage, some items reduce parry/dodge chance against you, spellcaster weapons grant spell damage and healing (per the Deep Dive recap). Dungeon drops are re-examined ("memorable items remain memorable," less exciting items adjusted), hundreds of new drops added, unique dungeon boss items are now blue quality, set bonuses improved, quest rewards expanded (e.g. Mor'ladim's Duskwood quest adds the Ladimore Heirloom Ring), world-drop epics improved, hundreds of new rare-creature drops (per the Deep Dive recap)</li>
            <li><strong>Talent trees</strong> — Classic structure retained (same number of rows) with one-point milestones at 11, 21 and 31 points plus a new 16-point milestone; some talents stay as patch 1.12, some are adjusted, and key class buffs (Divine Spirit, Blessing of Kings, Improved Mark of the Wild) are now baseline for their classes (per the Deep Dive recap). Zierhut (The Sun interview) adds the goal: all 27 talent trees viable for dungeons and raids ("everyone is somebody you'd be willing to bring along") but none so strong a raid would stack them</li>
            <li><strong>Mounts</strong> — the game adopts the Burning Crusade-style mount-cost approach: mount cost is shifted into training, and learning riding skill grants a mount (per the Deep Dive recap)</li>
            <li><strong>Leveling curve</strong> — the experience curve is unchanged; the new zones supply alternate paths through the ranges where Classic slows down. Dungeon design pushes players outdoors: monster-kill XP is brought "way down" while first-run dungeon-quest XP is "much, much, much higher," so repeated dungeon grinding loses to questing in Shen'dralas, the Riverglades or Hyjal (per Zierhut and Nuthals, The Sun interview)</li>
            <li><strong>Leveling combat stays Classic-paced</strong> — around levels 10–15 each kill takes 10–15 seconds, and pulling three mobs means "you're probably gonna die" (per Greenfield, MMORPG.com interview)</li>
            <li><strong>Chase drops keep old raids relevant</strong> — every boss gets low-drop-chance, very strong chase items ("every boss has a Dragonspine Trophy equivalent item on them, or two, or three"), and item progression is deliberately slowed so Blackwing-Lair-style obsolescence never happens: "everything at 60 stays relevant" (per Zierhut, The Sun interview)</li>
            <li><strong>Biome-specific gear effects</strong> — itemization's horizontal layer (per Greenfield, MMORPG.com interview, September 15; recap corroborated by MassivelyOP, September 16): "hundreds" of effects keyed to activities and threats — <strong>Whitemane's Chapeau</strong> makes resurrection spells cast faster, sets grant more damage against dragons — so older sets stay useful long past their tier (pugging a two-year-old raid tier to build "the best dragon-slaying set"). Raid tiers step up in nominal rather than dramatic power, learning from how Naxx-era gear optimized everything; epics "maintain that prestige" while a blue item can be "better for a specific purpose" than an epic</li>
            <li><strong>Storage grows, exact size disputed</strong> — Greenfield estimates the default bank much larger ("96 slots or something like that... either twice or four times as large"); beta reporting separately reads a 48-slot bank, so treat the number as an open conflict until launch (per Greenfield, MMORPG.com interview; dev numbers are estimates). Bags start at the original sizes, with more storage options planned down the road</li>
            <li><strong>Loot stays a surprise</strong> — item stats only become visible once that item first drops on the server (for you or anyone), and the Dungeon Journal initially leaves encounters unexplained, so the meta is discovered collectively rather than from pre-built BiS lists (per Senior Game Designer Josh Greenfield, MMORPG.com interview; recap corroborated by MassivelyOP, September 16)</li>
<li><strong>Beta datamine round one</strong> — classicwowforever's read of the beta client (build 1.60.1.69893, September 17) backs the surprise-loot design in the files: item records now carry only stat types plus basis-point weights (10,000 = 100%) with exact amounts kept server-side (verified byte-exact across all 19,119 readable records; resistances are stat types 51–56; Thunderfury's raw file weights read 2174/3478/3478/3913). Roughly 66 items, 872 spells, 90 quests and ~3,300 files are double-encrypted with all 8 keys withheld from the client, and the boss-drop table is empty (208 bytes, zero records) — so no datamining can say which boss drops what. 4,862 new items and 3,472 new spells are readable: full detail in the [round-one datamine](/news/forever-beta-datamine-round-one/). Pre-release data, AI-assisted per classicwowforever's caveat</li>
<li><strong>Stronghold and City magic</strong> — four spelled-out effects (maximum health, Block, Dodge, and healing/damage from all magical spells) are doubled inside Strongholds and Cities in the beta spell data (Bastion of Tyranny, Stabilized, Toy Soldier, Black Horn Necklace) — "defending territory will feel tangibly stronger" (per classicwowforever's beta datamine)</li>
<li><strong>Reported class changes (demo captures, unconfirmed)</strong> — Priest: <strong>Divine Spirit</strong> now baseline (official) and <strong>Fear Ward</strong> available to all Priests (no longer Dwarf-only, per the demo), with race-specific Priest spells reworked (Dwarf Chastise/Desperate Prayer, Gnome Confounding Flash/Contingency Plan, Human Divine Grace/Feedback, Night Elf Elune's Grace/Starshards, Troll Hex of Weakness/Shadowguard, Undead Dark Sacrifice/Touch of Weakness). Warrior: <strong>Victory Rush</strong> baseline (damage and a heal worth 10% of max health after a kill), Weaponmaster merges the Arms weapon talent into one, Bloodthirst lowered 45%→35% of Attack Power, Enrage triggers on any damage taken (25%→10%), TBC-imported Precision/Focused Rage/Vitality talents, Recklessness/Retaliation/Shield Wall no longer share a cooldown, Slam gains a 15s cooldown, Shield Block blocks 2 attacks, Thunder Clap usable in Defensive Stance. Mage (mostly reported; Arcane Blast and Hot Streak verified): Arcane Blast milestone (stacks to 4, +10% other-spell damage per stack), Missile Barrage, Ice Lance (300% vs Frozen), Blast Wave, Pyroblast, Fingers of Frost, Shatter now 3 ranks, new Frostfire Bolt. Shaman (reported): Lava Burst, Rage of the Farseer and Riptide capstones — plus <strong>Dwarf Shaman and Horde Skyborne Shaman confirmed</strong> (no Alliance Skyborne Shaman). Hunter, Rogue and Warlock have no detailed changes yet (all per wowforeverclassic's class pages, September 17, graded reported)</li>
<li><strong>First Aid crafts healing potions</strong> — the beta moves the healing-potion line out of Alchemy and into First Aid: Minor (skill 55), Lesser (85), Healing (135), Greater (155, Manual), Superior (215, Manual), Major (275, Manual) — now needing cooking spices like Mild and Hot Spices alongside herbs and vials (per Icy Veins, September 17; single-source, skill levels datamined — full [post](/news/forever-first-aid-healing-potions/))</li>
        </ul>
    <h3>Playing together in a realmless Azeroth</h3>
        <ul class="plain-list">
            <li><strong>Realmless:</strong> players choose a ruleset (<strong>Normal, PvP, Roleplaying or Hardcore</strong>) instead of a named realm; Hardcore arrives after launch</li>
            <li>Each ruleset is one large ecosystem; <strong>cross-ruleset grouping is unsupported</strong>, factions remain separate, and dungeons/raids require the same ruleset — though battlegrounds may mix rulesets except Hardcore, which stays separate (per Blizzard's ruleset post, September 21)</li>
            <li><strong>PvP ruleset locks you to one faction</strong> — on the PvP mega-server you play Horde <em>or</em> Alliance, not both; Blizzard cites faction identity, anti-scouting and balance, with SoD-style character-creation restriction planned if one faction outweighs the other (per the Game Informer interview)</li>
<li><strong>No GDKP</strong> — GDKP raiding is not allowed in Forever; Blizzard cites preserving the social/guild experience, says gold buying and gold selling will be combated more aggressively, and points to Season of Discovery as a positive no-GDKP experiment with ongoing economy monitoring. Icy Veins' transcription of Greenfield's full answer adds that GDKP "is an excellent way for bad actors to launder large sums of gold," with accounts of buyers, sellers and bots actioned and the economy monitored "very, very actively" (September 17 Q&A recap)</li>
<li><strong>No group finder or summoning stones</strong> — Blizzard confirmed neither is coming; social discovery stays manual (per Warcraft Tavern, September 17)</li>
<li><strong>Waylaid Supplies and the ACA return</strong> — Waylaid crates/supplies and the Azeroth Commerce Authority and Durotar Supply and Logistics factions from Season of Discovery appear in Forever's beta (per Warcraft Tavern's September 17 coverage and Blizzard's own known-issues list)</li>
            <li>Characters use <strong>two-part names unique per region</strong>; language preferences (24-hour change cooldown, account-wide) and the "<strong>Familiar Faces</strong>" system aim to preserve community at scale (per the Deep Dive recap and Blizzard's ruleset post)</li>
            <li><strong>Hardcore death transfer:</strong> a dying Hardcore character can transfer to another ruleset, including PvP — progress flows out of Hardcore but nothing flows back in (per the Deep Dive recap and Wowhead)</li>
<li><strong>Character Transfer service</strong> — once you pick a ruleset, switching to another style requires the (as-yet-undetailed) Character Transfer service (September 17 Q&A recap)</li>
<li><strong>Ahn'Qiraj: not at launch, but planned</strong> — "no surprise drop" of AQ on release, while developers hinted at big plans for global events with Ahn'Qiraj as an example (September 17 Q&A recap)</li>
<li><strong>Oceanic</strong> — Oceanic players will predominantly play on Australian servers; occasional cross-region grouping can raise ping (September 17 Q&A recap)</li>
        </ul>
    <h3>Camping</h3>
        <ul class="plain-list">
            <li>A <strong>Basic Campfire</strong> creates a campsite: rest for useful benefits, contribute crafted objects, refresh food buffs</li>
            <li>Up to three nearby players can each add one object; professions learn their first campsite objects at <strong>skill 20</strong>, and dungeon-boss <strong>Blueprints</strong> unlock more advanced objects</li>
            <li>Cooking upgrades camps to five or ten objects; Forever adds <strong>600+ recipes</strong> plus crafted food with experience or stat benefits (per the Deep Dive recap and Wowhead)</li>
            <li>Named camp objects (per wowforeverclassic, demo footage): Blacksmithing <strong>Sharpening Wheel</strong> (skill 20, Attack Power), <strong>Anvil</strong> (140) and <strong>Master Forge</strong> (300) crafting stations; Tailoring <strong>Faction Banner</strong> (Spirit); Herbalism <strong>Incense Candle</strong> (Intellect); Alchemy <strong>Alchemy Lab</strong> (reported: flask crafting at camp); Leatherworking <strong>Tanning Rack</strong>; First Aid <strong>First Aid Kit</strong> (Stamina, like Power Word: Fortitude; crafted from Linen Bandages and Refreshing Spring Water). Sitting (/sit) at a camp grants a one-hour buff and objects share a one-hour cooldown, one object per player</li>
            <li>Camp buff exclusivity and Favor economy (per Icy Veins beta scan, September 21, beta-graded): all 12 professions place buff objects exclusive with the matching class buff (Alchemy Mana/5 vs Blessing of Wisdom, Mining AP vs Might, Fishing 8% stats vs Kings, and so on down the list); buffs scale with level and upgraded objects allow crafting at camp. Waylaid crates turn in at Redridge (Alliance) and the Barrens (Horde) for <strong>Merchant's Favor</strong>, which buys level-20 rare helm recipes (30 Favor each, BoP) among 15+ headpieces</li>
            <li>Placement and pacing (per Greenfield, MMORPG.com interview, September 15; corroborated by MassivelyOP): campfires and their fixtures last <strong>about 10 minutes</strong> and each placement is on a <strong>one-hour cooldown</strong> (matching the one-hour cooldown in wowforeverclassic's demo read); you can plant almost anywhere except cities, with a minimum distance between fires ("we don't want shanty towns to pop up"); basic campfires hold three fixtures and higher-level camps hold more; camps are <strong>level-restricted</strong> and cheap to enter. A better-quality object simply replaces a weaker one ("the rinky dink sharpening stone"), so no profession can gate camps behind an elite build, and a "you smell an inviting fire" buff nudges players toward nearby campsites. Placement can be dangerous — a developer's Duskwood campfire got her killed by Stitches. Greenfield hedges the 10-minute duration with "I think": treat timers as estimates until beta confirms</li>
        </ul>
    <h3>Transmog</h3>
        <ul class="plain-list">
            <li>Opt-in; bind-on-pickup <strong>Uncommon or Rare dungeon drops</strong> grant their appearance to all eligible looters, so upgrade hunters and transmog collectors do not compete</li>
            <li><strong>Epic raid</strong> appearances go only to the player who receives the bound item; same-armor-type rules and level restrictions apply, and level 60 lifts restrictions for valid collection items (per the Deep Dive recap). PvP appearances additionally require the matching PvP rank, and saved outfits can hold several pieces per slot (per wowforeverclassic, official)</li>
            <li><strong>Toggle only hides others</strong> — turning transmog off affects only what you see on other players; your own configured outfit stays visible, and hiding your helmet or cloak remains disallowed as in vanilla (per Greenfield, MMORPG.com interview)</li>
        </ul>
    <h3>Design pillars</h3>
            <ul class="plain-list">
                <li>Approachable and familiar</li>
                <li>The world as the main character</li>
                <li>World changes stay <strong>additive, not destructive</strong> — situational terrain changes are possible for stories but kept "sparing," with Cataclysm's ambition-as-cost cited as the cautionary tale; Azshara gained quests and Wetlands got new lighting (per Greenfield and Parrott, MMORPG.com interview)</li>
                <li>The journey before the destination</li>
                <li>Protecting and nurturing the social experiences of Azeroth</li>
                <li><strong>No flying mounts. No level scaling.</strong> Effort matters; rewards come from playing</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="panel cp-span-full">
    <h2>New Race and Class Combinations (Pre-Release Preview)</h2>
    <p>Blizzard confirmed new race and class combinations as a design goal. The Deep Dive recap officially names <strong>six new combinations</strong>: <strong>Gnome Priest, Human Hunter, Dwarf Shaman, Orc Mage, Troll Warlock, and Undead Paladin</strong> (with more planned for future updates). Captures from the BlizzCon pre-release build (transcribed by classicwowforever.com, a fan wiki that logs its sources) show which combinations are new plus redesigned racials. Everything here is pre-release data observed in a September 12 build, not a launch guarantee.</p>
    <h3>New race and class combinations</h3>
